    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.grammar_units, $seed${"slug":"a1-and-but-or-in-picture-notes","title":"And, but and or in picture notes","level":"A1","status":"published","sort_order":340,"metadata":{},"focus_area":"and-but-or","explanation_text":"And adds one thing to another. “I can see a boat and a house” means I can see both. You can use and to join actions too: “I pick up the pencil and draw a boat.”\n\nBut adds something that is different from what you may expect. “The room is small, but it is bright” gives two details that pull in different directions. But can connect two short sentences when both ideas are true.\n\nOr gives a choice. “Draw a boat or a house” asks you to choose a picture. “Is the pencil blue or green?” asks which of two colors it is. In these practice tasks, the words both, surprising detail and choose help you decide which joining word to use.\n\nThe examples compare a note with a joining word that does not match the message and a corrected note.","contrast_text":"“Take the pencil and the pen” asks for both. “Take the pencil or the pen” gives you a choice in this context. “The pen is old, but it still works” gives a contrast.","common_error_pattern":"Choose and for an addition, but for a contrast, or or for a choice. Read the whole message before you choose the joining word.","examples_json":[{"bad":"Take both things. Take the brush or the cup.","good":"Take both things. Take the brush and the cup."},{"bad":"Choose one color. Use red and yellow.","good":"Choose one color. Use red or yellow."},{"bad":"The pencil is short, or I can still use it.","good":"The pencil is short, but I can still use it."},{"bad":"I see a blue line but a red line. Both are here.","good":"I see a blue line and a red line. Both are here."}],"tags":["joining ideas","and","but","or"]}$seed$::jsonb) r
