    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.grammar_units, $seed${"slug":"a2-both-either-neither-with-two-choices","title":"Both, either and neither for two choices","level":"A2","status":"published","sort_order":340,"metadata":{},"focus_area":"both-either-neither","explanation_text":"Use both when a statement is true for the two things you are talking about. “Both stops have seats” means there are seats at stop A and at stop B. Both can go before a plural noun, as in both stops. You can also say both of the stops or both of them.\n\nUse either when one of two choices is fine. Say “You can use either gate” if there are two gates and anyone can choose one. Use a singular noun after either in this pattern. Say either of them when you are speaking about two things you already named.\n\nUse neither to rule out the two options together. “Neither gate is open” means the first gate is closed and the second gate is closed. Neither already gives a negative meaning, so use is open here. In these exercises, use the singular form is after neither plus a singular noun.","contrast_text":"“Both lockers are free” makes a statement about the two lockers. “Either locker is fine” gives a free choice. “Neither locker is free” rules out the two lockers.","common_error_pattern":"After both, use a plural noun in “both seats”. After either or neither, use a singular noun in “either seat” or “neither seat”.","examples_json":[{"bad":"Both ticket is valid.","good":"Both tickets are valid."},{"bad":"You can choose either routes.","good":"You can choose either route."},{"bad":"Neither ticket are valid.","good":"Neither ticket is valid."},{"bad":"Both of they have a blue cover.","good":"Both of them have a blue cover."}],"tags":["two options","both","either","neither"]}$seed$::jsonb) r
